Liz we want to talk about another big  story in the region this is North  Korea's rise in tension with the u.s. US  as you know says it that the military  fired three missiles yesterday and the  second launch in a week u.s. also  revealed that it seized a North Korean  cargo ship in violation of economic  sanctions this morning North Korea says  Kim jong-un is telling his military to  keep full combat posture their words so  what can you tell us about this well  let's start with those missile launches  yesterday - most analysts they look less  like a show of force than a grab for  attention North Korean state media  released these pictures showing a  gleeful kim jonghwan apparently watching  those missile tests yesterday it's the  second drill he's observed in the past  week North Korean state media avoided  specifying what the weapons were but the  US military says they were three  ballistic missiles  not enough said president Trump to ruin  his relationship with Kim jong-un  they were smaller missiles and  short-range missiles nobody's happy  about it but we'll take it a good look  and we'll see we'll say the relationship  continues but we'll see what happens but  these are the first missile launches  since 2017 and a sure sign that Kim  jong-un is becoming frustrated after the  last summit with President Trump  collapsed however this week's launches  are not enough of a provocation to  derail future talks says geopolitical  analyst Thomas Anderson they certainly  are not going to prevent another summit  and it's not as if it is a  intercontinental range missile and it's  not the testing of a nuclear warhead  these are the two elements that Kim  jong-un promised he would put a  moratorium on technically that  moratorium still holds but Kim has  already warned the United States to  change its position or there will be no  third summit and ratcheting up tensions  between the two countries even further  the United States announced that it had  seized a huge North Korean cargo ship  off the coast of Indonesia  now that's the first time a US has  actually detained a Korean ship it  happened last April but it shows you  that the u.s. is very serious about  choking off any and all revenue going to  North Korea that could in fact be  funneled into developing its nuclear  program Nora we will continue to watch  that very very closely Elizabeth Palmer  in Beijing thank you so much
 

